#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295109 :question/problem "![](images/f4/f41ad0635fb7bb426a387f825097a31dddb591861012f2543336a142832df9db.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of $△ABC$ above is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B,$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=4, \\\\ a & =BC=8, \\\\ m∠B & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}=0.642\\,788,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (0.642\\hspace{0.167em}788) \\\\  & \\approx 10.3,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest tenth**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295110 :question/problem "![](images/68/68977537a5f180dd483534ed1415326251d4a76a3dd0833e9701487cd8b5b33d.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest tenth**, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}с & =AB=18, \\\\ b & =AC=12, \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=0.866\\,025,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot 18\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{120}^{∘} \\\\  & =108\\cdot (0.866\\hspace{0.167em}025…) \\\\  & \\approx 93.5\\hspace{0.167em}{\\text{ft}}^{2},\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to the **nearest tenth**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295111 :question/problem "![](images/54/54265327c77bedb300fe0d1f1f08ca3ab2d7ae37f35f49007d3f20ab3a21dbaa.png)\n\nRounded to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram $ABCD$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b1/b1d0cf7c5a7c6ad7f0f52c8686239920fabcb05be57372ae0c7c9c9491838a52.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACB} & =\\frac{1}{2}(10)(6)\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =30\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & \\approx 25.981,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathcal{A}}_{ABCD} & =2{\\mathcal{A}}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 25.981 \\\\  & =51.962.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSo, to the **nearest integer**, the area of the parallelogram is $52.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295123 :question/problem "![](images/20/200dcd87827ae9683ba0ecb3b284509b31d32f9e0dc1b9736a260d2f7d71b0a0.png)\n\nRounded to **two decimal places**, the area of $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}ac\\operatorname{sin}B,$$\n\nwhere $∠B$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $c.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=5, \\\\ a & =BC=5, \\\\ m∠B & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=0.866\\,025,\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n \n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =12.5\\cdot (0.866\\hspace{0.167em}025) \\\\  & \\approx 10.83,\\end{aligned}$$\n\nrounded to **two decimal places**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295125 :question/problem "![](images/86/866f869f43e2663c2f63a485049f84ae059e2fdcb73db0445eb355491957c66a.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ Then,  $AC=${{field-1}} $\\text{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={120}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{120}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 10\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =5b\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 25\\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} & =5b\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\ 5b & =25 \\\\ b & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=5\\,\\text{ cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295126 :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c0bf3060129e2a03dab0fcd2a6f0f6068d1ec565143b83d11ad4d8ae1b41b85.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the acute triangle $△ABC$ is $3{\\text{ mm}}^{2}.$ Then, the measure of $∠A$ is{{field-1}} ${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ mm}, \\\\ b & =AC=4\\,\\text{mm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $m∠A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathcal{A} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ 3 & =2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}A \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}A & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}A & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295127 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(3,-2,1),$ $C(-1,4,-1),$ and $D(2,1,0).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(8-1)+2(-4-1)+3(-2-4) \\\\  & =14-10-18 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-14|=14.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295128 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,-3,2),$ $B(4,-1,5),$ $C(-2,0,1),$ and $D(3,-2,3).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1)-2((-3)\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3((-3)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(3+1)-2(-3+2)+3(-3-6) \\\\  & =12+2-27 \\\\  & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-13|=13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295135 :question/problem "If $H=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-7\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-7(0-(-20))+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot (20)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-140-6 \\\\  & =-146\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295136 :question/problem "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2(0-0)-3(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295137 :question/problem "If $Q=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3)+2(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))+5(4\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =3(2)+2(8)+5(12+1) \\\\  & =6+16+5(13) \\\\  & =6+16+65 \\\\  & =87\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295138 :question/problem "If $R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))+2(4\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 6)+0 \\\\  & =1(6+5)+2(8-30) \\\\  & =11+2(-22) \\\\  & =11-44 \\\\  & =-33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295139 :question/problem "If $W=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =5\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5(4\\cdot 1-6\\cdot (-3))+2((-1)(1)-6\\cdot 2)+3((-1)(-3)-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =5(4+18)+2(-1-12)+3(3-8) \\\\  & =5(22)+2(-13)+3(-5) \\\\  & =110-26-15 \\\\  & =69\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295140 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(A)=37$, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(x\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)-(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(x-20)-(3+8) \\\\  & =2x-40-11 \\\\  & =2x-51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $37,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-51 & =37 \\\\ 2x & =88 \\\\ x & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295141 :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular.\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =\\begin{vmatrix}k+1 & 2k & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & k & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(k+1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2k\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(k+1)(5\\cdot 1-0\\cdot k)-2k(0\\cdot 1-0\\cdot -2)+3(0\\cdot k-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5)-2k(0)+3(10) \\\\  & =5k+5+30 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =-35 \\\\ k & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295151 :question/problem "Given that $G=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x-1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & x+1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ x+1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-(2x-1)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+6\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & x+1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(3\\cdot (-4)-0\\cdot (x+1))-(2x-1)(0)+6(0\\cdot (x+1)-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =x(-12)+0+6(3) \\\\  & =-12x+18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12x+18 & =0 \\\\ -12x & =-18 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295160 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is $32$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{2}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ x & -2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}x & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(-2\\cdot 0-(-1)(4))-3(x\\cdot 0-(-1)(1)) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2)(4)-3(1) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-8-3 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $32$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-11 & =32 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =43 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{43}{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{43}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{43}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295161 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is $40$, find all the possible values of $x$ **in ascending order**.\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let’s use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x & 5 \\\\ 3 & x & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 7)+5(3-7x) \\\\  & =x(2x-4)-2x(-22)+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-4x+44x+15-35x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+5x+15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $40$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5x+15 & =40 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+5x-25 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5\\pm \\sqrt{{5}^{2}-4(2)(-25)}}{2(2)} \\\\ x & =\\frac{-5+\\sqrt{225}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\pm 15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, **in ascending order**, the solutions are $x=-5$ and $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295164 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,1,-1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1-4-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295165 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨1,1,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨-3,2,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨4,-2,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-2))-1((-3)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 4)+2((-3)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2+3-4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|1|=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295166 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,5,0⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,0,6⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(30)-2(0)+3(-20) \\\\  & =30-0-60 \\\\  & =-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-30|=30.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295167 :question/problem "Compute the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨3,1,4⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨0,2,5⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨6,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume of a parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 0)-(0\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 6)+4(0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =6+30-48 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-12|=12.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295168 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{x}=⟨2,0,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨1,4,3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨0,2,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & k \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2)+0+k(1\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(20-6)+2k \\\\  & =28+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}\\cdot (\\mathbf{y}\\times \\mathbf{z}) & =0 \\\\ 28+2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-28 \\\\ k & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295169 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,3,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,4,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,k,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ k & 5\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot k)-3(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2)=(20-2k)+12 \\\\  & =32-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 32-2k & =0 \\\\ -2k & =-32 \\\\ k & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295170 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,k,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,3,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,0,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)-k(0\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)+k(0\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =15+8k-12k \\\\  & =15-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ 15-4k & =0 \\\\ 4k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{15}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295171 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨4,{k}^{2},2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,5,3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,1,0⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n**In ascending order:** $\\,$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}\n$\\,$\nand\n$\\,$\n$k=${{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & {k}^{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-{k}^{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 1)-{k}^{2}(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 2)+2(1\\cdot 1-5\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4(-3)-{k}^{2}(0-6)+2(1-10) \\\\  & =-12+6{k}^{2}-18 \\\\  & =6{k}^{2}-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet the triple product equal to 0:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{k}^{2}-30 & =0 \\\\ 6{k}^{2} & =30 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =5 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if \n$k=-\\sqrt{5}$ or $k=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295173 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(1,0,2),$ $B(3,4,5),$ $C(6,2,4),$ and $D(5,3,6).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,4,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(6\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,3,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3)-4(5\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 4)+3(5\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =2(8-6)-4(20-8)+3(15-8) \\\\  & =4-48+21 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-23|=23.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295174 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(3,0,2),$ $B(5,1,3),$ $C(4,3,5),$ and $D(3,0,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{k})-(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(3\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)-1(1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 0)+1(1\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2(9)-1(3)+1(0) \\\\  & =18-3+0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|15|=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295175 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(0,1,3),$ $B(0,5,5),$ $C(3,4,5),$ and $D(2,0,2).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,3,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k})-(1\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-1\\mathbf{j}-1\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4(3\\cdot -1-2\\cdot 2)+2(3\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =28-18 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|10|=10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295176 :question/problem "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD},$ given the points $A(-1,0,1),$ $B(2,-3,4),$ $C(-2,-2,2),$ and $D(0,-1,5).$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+1\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-8+1)+3(-4-1)+3(1-(-2)) \\\\  & =-21-15+9 \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-27|=27.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295221 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨3,4,-1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨2,-5,2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-5))\\mathbf{i}-(3\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(3\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(8-5)\\mathbf{i}-(6+2)\\mathbf{j}+(-15-8)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-8\\mathbf{j}-23\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-8,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295222 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨\\sqrt{3},2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot -1-0\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{j}+(\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+\\sqrt{3}\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\sqrt{3},-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295223 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨\\frac{2}{3},0,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((0)(-3)-(1)(2))\\mathbf{i}-(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(-3)-(1)(1))\\mathbf{j}+(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)(2)-(0)(1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2)\\mathbf{i}-(-2-1)\\mathbf{j}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,3,\\frac{4}{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295227 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-2\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+6\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-4)(6)-(1)(5))\\mathbf{i}-((3)(6)-(1)(-2))\\mathbf{j}+((3)(5)-(-4)(-2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-24-5)\\mathbf{i}-(18+2)\\mathbf{j}+(15-8)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-29\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+7\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295228 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-5\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-3\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 1-(-5)(4))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 1-(-5)(-3))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot (-3))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(2+20)\\mathbf{i}-(1-15)\\mathbf{j}+(4+6)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =22\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+14\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+10\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295229 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=-6\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot -1)\\mathbf{i}-(-6\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+(-6\\cdot -1-3\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(15+2)\\mathbf{i}-(-30-8)\\mathbf{j}+(6-12)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =17\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+38\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295230 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{i}-7\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=5\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-7)(-6)-(4)(3))\\mathbf{i}-((2)(-6)-(4)(5))\\mathbf{j}+((2)(3)-(-7)(5))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(42-12)\\mathbf{i}-(-12-20)\\mathbf{j}+(6+35)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =30\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+32\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+41\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295231 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k}.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}-\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-3}}$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0)(-2)-(3)(2)\\mathbf{i}-(-4)(-2)-(3)(1)\\mathbf{j}+(-4)(2)-(0)(1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-6)\\mathbf{i}-(8-3)\\mathbf{j}+(-8-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295235 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2x,3,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-2x,0,4⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{280}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 3 & 1 \\\\ -2x & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ -2x & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 3 \\\\ -2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(12-0)\\mathbf{i}-(8x+2x)\\mathbf{j}+(0+6x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨12,-10x,6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}+(-10x)^{2}+(6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+100{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ \\sqrt{136{x}^{2}+144} & =\\sqrt{280} \\\\ 136{x}^{2}+144 & =280 \\\\ 136{x}^{2} & =136 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-1,$ $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295236 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,0,1⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-x,2,0⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{29}.$ Then, in **ascending order**, the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 0 & 1 \\\\ -x & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 1 \\\\ -x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ -x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-2)\\mathbf{i}-(0+x)\\mathbf{j}+(2x+0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,-x,2x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-x)^{2}+(2x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{x}^{2}+4} & =\\sqrt{29} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+4 & =29 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"}]}
